# The seam fixtures are encoder output, compared byte-for-byte by
# `tests/test_seam_fixture.py` and parsed byte-for-byte by
# `web/tests/seam.test.ts`. This repo is developed on Windows with
# `core.autocrlf=true`, where git rewrites line endings in any file it
# *guesses* is text.
#
# Mangling one of these blobs takes two things at once: no NUL byte early
# enough for git's guess to come out right, AND an 0x0A or 0x0D byte for it to
# rewrite. Today's fixtures carry neither, so they are safe twice over -- but
# both are properties of the data currently in them, not of the format. A
# regenerated fixture holding a 10% or 13% probability byte, or a support
# count of 10 or 13, introduces the second one immediately, and a roster that
# happened to place a NUL later introduces the first. The guard is cheap and
# the failure it prevents is a byte-identity test failing on a machine whose
# author changed nothing.
web/tests/fixtures/*.bin binary

# Deliberately NOT extended to the two text fixtures beside them,
# `seam.json` and `week-buckets.json`. Those are checked out CRLF here and
# that is fine, in both directions: Python reads them through
# `Path.read_text`, whose universal newlines turn CRLF back into LF before
# either is compared against a freshly rendered string, and `JSON.parse`
# tolerates CRLF outright. Pinning them to `eol=lf` would churn two tracked
# files to fix nothing.
